            <ArticleTitle>Clinical prediction for success of interventions for managing low back pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>463-79, ix-x</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.csm.2008.03.002</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Treatment-based classification, one approach to subgrouping patients with &quot;nonspecific&quot; lower back pain, focuses on identifying clusters of findings from the history and clinical examination that predict a more favorable outcome with a specific treatment approach. By matching patients with the appropriate specific exercise, stabilization exercise, spinal manipulation, or traction treatment, providers may expect a high probability of a successful clinical outcome. This article reviews the evidence for various interventions commonly used in the treatment of lower back pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
